{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1085644-majority-of-u-s-high-schools-aren-t-as-bad-as-it-might-seemed","id":1085644,"title":"Majority of U.S High Schools aren't as bad as it might seemed","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Additional Discussion","Off-Topic"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-04-29T18:49:43-07:00","id":1085644,"title":"Majority of U.S High Schools aren't as bad as it might seemed","content":"A little background about myself. I am a first generation Chinese American that was born to immigrant parents. We didn't have much so I pretty much grew up in poverty. My parents could barely speak any English which made it a lot tougher to get better paying jobs than the ones they had. Ex: I didn't get a cell phone until my junior year of High School.<br /> <br /> The High School that I went to wasn't the best and I witnessed things that I found was pretty disturbing. There were quite a lot of disciplinary problems with the students. Teachers often have to use delicate class time to discipline students because there were so many students were just messing around instead of paying attention.<br /> <br /> I was kind of quiet. I rarely gave my teachers any trouble and I always tried to do the best I can. Every day after school I would try my best to focus on my studies. I dedicated around 1-2 hours studying everyday after school. Even if there was no homework, I would review the lesson that was taught and lessons that were going to be taught. More time would be spent studying if there was going to be an exam the next day. This means doing my homework and reviewing future chapters that were going to be taught in class. This made me a lot more prepared.<br /> <br /> Many students half-a** their homework and rarely allocated much time for their studies. I maintained a 3.8 GPA throughout High School. I wasn't naturally bright or well-off. I was struggling like many other students yet I accomplish much better academically than those students.<br /> <br /> That's why I would say the education system in the U.S isn't as bad as most people think it is. I really think the entirely of the blame shouldn't shift to the education system but it should be at least 50-50 with the students on the other side.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1945-magicballz","name":"Magicballz","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"175"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-29T19:24:40-07:00","id":1085651,"title":"","content":"The biggest issue is parents. Too many parents just view school as day care. It's up to the teachers and the school to handle everything, and they shouldn't need to do a thing. When parents are contacted, they feel like the school isn't doing its job, so they already come in with a combative attitude. Parents often take the side of their children, insisting that their kid would <i>never</i> do something like tell a teacher to fuck off, and the teacher is just singling out their kid. <br /> <br /> And the problem is, when parents feel more interested in fighting against the teachers than in disciplining their kids, you naturally end up with students who are constantly stepping out of line and making it harder for the teacher to their job and teach.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-29T21:05:43-07:00","id":1085677,"title":"","content":"Oh boy...<br /> <br /> First off, lets be clear. There's no single problem with the US public school system. There are lots of issues, and most of them are impossible to simply cut out like a tumor. Everything is interconnected into a Gordian knot of issues that takes an obnoxious amount of effort and work to address fully (which is basically why it never happens and things consistently slide to the worst).<br /> <br /> Blaming the kids though, is pretty much the most useless thing to do. Teenagers especially are pretty much in the worst stage of their biological development for traditional education models. Regardless of relative intelligence (however you want to define it) being good at high school is largely a genetic crap shoot of whether or not you luck out with the right order of development and personality that meshes with traditional education. And that's before you even get into socioeconomic issues (both personal to the family, and the community at large).<br /> <br /> Parents... yeah, parents definitely have some blame going on. Of course, part of a parents role is to advocate for their child. Expecting them to do less is silly, if not insulting. Most parents also don't unquestionably side with their kids, the side with their idealized version of who their kids are. That's really the core to most issues with parents. Sometimes you get what <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar741_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"741\" data-vbnamecard=\"741\">AnubisXy</a> was saying. Sometimes you get parents that wonder why the teachers are wasting their time with their &quot;screw up&quot; kid. There's also an issue of engagement. Some parents are really engaged with their kid's education, and some aren't. A lot try to be without learning how to do so effectively, because as a society we just sort of expect parents to know that even though it's not that simple.<br /> <br /> Then there's all the institutional crap. The School Boards vs. the State DoE vs the Federal DoE. A complete lack of unified educational philosophy, sluggish implementation of new research in education, and failure to make proper use of the charter school system to provide for students that need different learning environments (made worse by the wild and random charter standards by state).<br /> <br /> Then there's things like how we fund public schools via real estate taxes, ensuring the poorer areas have less educational resources than wealthier ones, and higher level funding doesn't actually get used to even this out (and focuses on means testing over getting annoyed a schools that use external funding to justify using their normal funding on non-academic spending).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-29T21:38:32-07:00","id":1085688,"title":"","content":"^ Well how do you explain why many East Asian Americans are so successful in school then? You can still deal with a not so great school with a strong enough work ethics.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://www.pnas.org/content/111/23/8416\" target=\"_blank\">http://www.pnas.org/content/111/23/8416</a><br /> <br /> <b>&quot;The authors wrote that family wealth was not a factor in performance.&quot;</b><br /> <br /> <b>&quot;The poverty rates of Chinese and Vietnamese are higher than they are for whites. However the disadvantaged children of Chinese and Vietnamese immigrant families routinely surpass the educational attainment of their native-born, middle-class white peers.&quot;</b>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1945-magicballz","name":"Magicballz","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"175"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-29T21:52:46-07:00","id":1085691,"title":"","content":"I very nearly failed out of high school due to lack of adequate mental healthcare for students and was essentially talked out of a career of education when the staff unanimously told me NOT to pursue because of how they were treated, and this was at a nicer school.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Remi. she/her. game designer.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/281-atamajakki","name":"atamajakki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar281_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5487"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-29T22:21:22-07:00","id":1085703,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Magicballz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1085644-majority-of-u-s-high-schools-aren-t-as-bad-as-it-might-seemed?p=1085688#post1085688\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You can still deal with a not so great school with a strong enough work ethics.</div> </div> </div> </div>Doesn't that suggest that work ethic has more to do with it than what the high school is like? Even your link supports that, 'We find that the Asian-American educational advantage over whites is attributable mainly to Asian students exerting greater academic effort...' Of course, that does support the idea that it's not the schools that are flawed as much as the students attending them. And instilling a strong work ethic in their children is something a lot of American families have had a hard time with and is causing trouble for many businesses like farming.<br /> <br /> I'm happy your school experience was a good one.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-30T00:11:17-07:00","id":1085723,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Magicballz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1085644-majority-of-u-s-high-schools-aren-t-as-bad-as-it-might-seemed?p=1085688#post1085688\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">^ Well how do you explain why many East Asian Americans are so successful in school then? You can still deal with a not so great school with a strong enough work ethics.</div> </div> </div> </div>Um, basically exactly what your own link points out: culture.<br /> <br /> A lot of the cultural groups with a statistical above average performance have retained cultural values that generate better results in the system.<br /> <br /> One of the things I discussed was parental engagement. East Asian cultures that have been transposed to the US tend to encourage a high degree of parental involvement in their children's education. It's a cultural value to learn what the education system rewards, and take steps to raise their children to work towards those specific rewards. While I don't particularly like the phrase, there's a reason why &quot;Tiger Mom&quot; exists.<br /> <br /> That's why it transcends cognitive testing or socio-economic factors.<br /> <br /> In the studies that look at the Jewish population you find similar higher level performance across those lines. There's a cultural value in the demographic at hand.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-30T05:22:25-07:00","id":1085758,"title":"","content":"This also does not include the fact that the standard for education overall has dropped - in all levels of education. Nowadays, every student needs to be &quot;special&quot; and every child is a success. Due to this mentality - a kid will rarely be held back, like say they aren't on the same reading level as the other students because it would hurt the child socially or deeply or whatnot. The education system creates students without many critical thinking skills, leading to much of its application being useless. This also doesn't include the fact of that universities need to get their shit together as degrees are commodities that everyone needs to consume hence leading to a generation of slackers. (Not to say I'm better or worse from it - just my thoughts on the matter as someone in university :P )","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11457-volke","name":"Volke","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11457_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"127"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-30T07:46:44-07:00","id":1085782,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Volke</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1085644-majority-of-u-s-high-schools-aren-t-as-bad-as-it-might-seemed?p=1085758#post1085758\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This also does not include the fact that the standard for education overall has dropped - in all levels of education. Nowadays, every student needs to be &quot;special&quot; and every child is a success. Due to this mentality - a kid will rarely be held back, like say they aren't on the same reading level as the other students because it would hurt the child socially or deeply or whatnot. The education system creates students without many critical thinking skills, leading to much of its application being useless. This also doesn't include the fact of that universities need to get their shit together as degrees are commodities that everyone needs to consume hence leading to a generation of slackers. (Not to say I'm better or worse from it - just my thoughts on the matter as someone in university :P )</div> </div> </div> </div>I think this post merits more critical thinking. You should definitely refer to Heavy Arms's posts and think about those critically.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I am no longer participating in the community. Please do not contact me about my previous work.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/159-leetsepeak","name":"Leetsepeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-30T09:56:39-07:00","id":1085813,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Volke</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1085644-majority-of-u-s-high-schools-aren-t-as-bad-as-it-might-seemed?p=1085758#post1085758\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This also does not include the fact that the standard for education overall has dropped - in all levels of education. Nowadays, every student needs to be &quot;special&quot; and every child is a success. Due to this mentality - a kid will rarely be held back, like say they aren't on the same reading level as the other students because it would hurt the child socially or deeply or whatnot.</div> </div> </div> </div>It's not so much that. Rather, remember that funding to public schools is determined by a schools pass/fail rate. And then remember that the administration of a school or district gets compensation for meeting the government mandated pass/fail rate and, conversely, if a school does not meet that standard the district could lose funding or worse, the government could step in and fire every single person working at that school including the janitors and lunch ladies - this actually happens. <br /> <br /> This means that there is massive pressure from the administration for teachers to pass students, even if those students need to be held back, because the principal and district leaders want their fat year-end monetary bonus so they can buy a new car or whatever, and they don't want their schools to go below standards. Teachers who choose to fail a student anyway often have to spend a lot of time justifying doing so. And if a teacher fails too many students, then the teacher is held accountable and will often find herself threatened with a loss of job. <br /> <br /> How this often plays out is, teachers are given an unofficial limit on how many students they're allowed to fail. If a teacher fails more students than that, then the teacher ends up facing some kind of retribution - poor marks on evaluations with the threat of being fired the next year if they don't &quot;make sure more students pass&quot; is a common one. Teachers who don't learn to play ball (or who refuse to promote students who don't deserve it) don't end up working in the profession for every long.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-30T10:23:08-07:00","id":1085818,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>AnubisXy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1085644-majority-of-u-s-high-schools-aren-t-as-bad-as-it-might-seemed?p=1085813#post1085813\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> It's not so much that. Rather, remember that funding to public schools is determined by a schools pass/fail rate. And then remember that the administration of a school or district gets compensation for meeting the government mandated pass/fail rate and, conversely, if a school does not meet that standard the district could lose funding or worse, the government could step in and fire every single person working at that school including the janitors and lunch ladies - this actually happens.<br /> <br /> This means that there is massive pressure from the administration for teachers to pass students, even if those students need to be held back, because the principal and district leaders want their fat year-end monetary bonus so they can buy a new car or whatever, and they don't want their schools to go below standards. Teachers who choose to fail a student anyway often have to spend a lot of time justifying doing so. And if a teacher fails too many students, then the teacher is held accountable and will often find herself threatened with a loss of job.<br /> <br /> How this often plays out is, teachers are given an unofficial limit on how many students they're allowed to fail. If a teacher fails more students than that, then the teacher ends up facing some kind of retribution - poor marks on evaluations with the threat of being fired the next year if they don't &quot;make sure more students pass&quot; is a common one. Teachers who don't learn to play ball (or who refuse to promote students who don't deserve it) don't end up working in the profession for every long.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> I understand that it's not necessarily the teacher's fault by any means - I understand they face pressure from multiple directions. It's just a fact that is leading to the downfall of education. Rather than it being something that is an an achievement, it's a requirement for a standard of living.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11457-volke","name":"Volke","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11457_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"127"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-30T10:29:48-07:00","id":1085819,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>AnubisXy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1085644-majority-of-u-s-high-schools-aren-t-as-bad-as-it-might-seemed?p=1085813#post1085813\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And if a teacher fails too many students, then the teacher is held accountable and will often find herself threatened with a loss of job.</div> </div> </div> </div>Further complicating this is the fact that public school teachers are government employees, and getting fired as a government employee makes it incredibly difficult to be hired in other government positions. That means a teacher being fired, for any reason, may not be able to teach in public schools any more, and may need to find a different career path. This creates a strong incentive for teachers to quit their jobs pre-emptively if they believe they are in danger of losing them.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-30T10:49:08-07:00","id":1085824,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Volke</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1085644-majority-of-u-s-high-schools-aren-t-as-bad-as-it-might-seemed?p=1085818#post1085818\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I understand that it's not necessarily the teacher's fault by any means - I understand they face pressure from multiple directions. It's just a fact that is leading to the downfall of education. Rather than it being something that is an an achievement, it's a requirement for a standard of living.</div> </div> </div> </div>The education system in America started changing big time after the Supreme Court ruled that &quot;tracking&quot; students was unconstitutional. Prior to that (mainly back during the Boomer generation) schools would track different students into different areas. Those whom the schools felt would be able to go to college were tracked into pre-college classes. Those who weren't, were given vocational studies.<br /> <br /> Which might not seem like a bad idea on the surface, but what was actually happening was that minorities would be primarily be tracked into vocational classes while the college tracks were almost entirely filled with white kids. The goal, of course, was to keep minorities out of college. Which was racist as fuck and why the Supreme Court ruled it was unconstitutional.<br /> <br /> But after they were told they couldn't do that anymore, schools didn't really know what to do, so they just put all students on the &quot;same&quot; track - the college one. Unfortunately at this point we ended up with the &quot;one-size fits all, everyone has to meet the exact same standards&quot; education system. And one size doesn't fit everyone, so you started having more and more students dropping out of school because they couldn't handle the material. In turn, this crisis made the government and the American people feel that something needed to be done. But nobody can really agree on what to do or how to do it. So they started pushing &quot;teacher accountability,&quot; with the goal of holding teachers accountable if students couldn't pass.<br /> <br /> But this runs into its own problems of course, more teachers leave the profession, and this means class sizes keep getting larger, and with bigger classes teachers have less time to devote to individual students, so they end up just promoting kids to the next grade level who shouldn't be moving up.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> What America needs to do is collectively decide on what the goal of the public education system should be, but that's simply not going to happen. It's too big of a subject and there's no easy answers. And worst of all, the big thing right now is vouchers, which are a terrible idea.<br /> <br /> Private schools don't have to follow of the same rules that public schools do. If a student misbehaves at a private school, they just throw that student out. If a student can't hack the material, they just throw him out. This means that private schools often have better statistics than public schools, because if anyone can't meet their standards, they're not in school anymore. Public schools meanwhile, have to take everyone and do their best to educate those students.<br /> <br /> So what's going to happen is, the good students who can perform well are going to end up in private schools. The underperforming students who can't won't be accepted into those private schools and will remain in public schools. Which means, with fewer high performing students, the overall statistics of public schools will drop even further. Basically the public schools will be left with the &quot;dregs&quot; (though I hate using that term) and worse, without those hardworking kids to set an example in public schools, you'll see even more students who slack off and don't take education seriously.<br /> <br /> If voucher programs succeed, I think it'll be the deathknell for the public education system in America, and you'll see a generation or two worth of students being left behind, stuck in failing public schools. And let's be honest - who will be going to private schools? Mostly white kids. And who will be stuck in those failing public schools? Minorities.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-30T14:51:04-07:00","id":1085900,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>AnubisXy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1085644-majority-of-u-s-high-schools-aren-t-as-bad-as-it-might-seemed?p=1085824#post1085824\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> The education system in America started changing big time after the Supreme Court ruled that &quot;tracking&quot; students was unconstitutional. Prior to that (mainly back during the Boomer generation) schools would track different students into different areas. Those whom the schools felt would be able to go to college were tracked into pre-college classes. Those who weren't, were given vocational studies.<br /> <br /> Which might not seem like a bad idea on the surface, but what was actually happening was that minorities would be primarily be tracked into vocational classes while the college tracks were almost entirely filled with white kids. The goal, of course, was to keep minorities out of college. Which was racist as fuck and why the Supreme Court ruled it was unconstitutional.<br /> <br /> But after they were told they couldn't do that anymore, schools didn't really know what to do, so they just put all students on the &quot;same&quot; track - the college one. Unfortunately at this point we ended up with the &quot;one-size fits all, everyone has to meet the exact same standards&quot; education system. And one size doesn't fit everyone, so you started having more and more students dropping out of school because they couldn't handle the material. In turn, this crisis made the government and the American people feel that something needed to be done. But nobody can really agree on what to do or how to do it. So they started pushing &quot;teacher accountability,&quot; with the goal of holding teachers accountable if students couldn't pass.<br /> <br /> But this runs into its own problems of course, more teachers leave the profession, and this means class sizes keep getting larger, and with bigger classes teachers have less time to devote to individual students, so they end up just promoting kids to the next grade level who shouldn't be moving up.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> What America needs to do is collectively decide on what the goal of the public education system should be, but that's simply not going to happen. It's too big of a subject and there's no easy answers. And worst of all, the big thing right now is vouchers, which are a terrible idea.<br /> <br /> Private schools don't have to follow of the same rules that public schools do. If a student misbehaves at a private school, they just throw that student out. If a student can't hack the material, they just throw him out. This means that private schools often have better statistics than public schools, because if anyone can't meet their standards, they're not in school anymore. Public schools meanwhile, have to take everyone and do their best to educate those students.<br /> <br /> So what's going to happen is, the good students who can perform well are going to end up in private schools. The underperforming students who can't won't be accepted into those private schools and will remain in public schools. Which means, with fewer high performing students, the overall statistics of public schools will drop even further. Basically the public schools will be left with the &quot;dregs&quot; (though I hate using that term) and worse, without those hardworking kids to set an example in public schools, you'll see even more students who slack off and don't take education seriously.<br /> <br /> If voucher programs succeed, I think it'll be the deathknell for the public education system in America, and you'll see a generation or two worth of students being left behind, stuck in failing public schools. And let's be honest - who will be going to private schools? Mostly white kids. And who will be stuck in those failing public schools? Minorities.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well I appreciate your thoughts on the matter - I'll be honest and say I didn't know all the facts or look at it like that. It's a shame that things like race are such a large part of the US education system :/, I honestly don't know how racism is still a thing in this day in age. *Shrugs* Maybe I just don't know enough - but I appreciate the perspective Anubis <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11457-volke","name":"Volke","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11457_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"127"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-30T15:23:44-07:00","id":1085915,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Volke</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1085644-majority-of-u-s-high-schools-aren-t-as-bad-as-it-might-seemed?p=1085900#post1085900\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well I appreciate your thoughts on the matter - I'll be honest and say I didn't know all the facts or look at it like that. It's a shame that things like race are such a large part of the US education system :/, I honestly don't know how racism is still a thing in this day in age. *Shrugs* Maybe I just don't know enough - but I appreciate the perspective Anubis <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></div> </div> </div> </div>Sadly, racism is still a thing in the US, across all parts of society, and the education system is no different. I have a minor in education and spent 2 years as a teacher before I realized how utterly screwed up everything is and left the profession. My first job I was working at a school that was 80% African-American and it lasted for all of 3 days. The first day I was talking with the principal, who was white, and mentioned I grew up in El Paso, Texas. She looks at me and says point blank, &quot;What did it feel like, being surrounded by spics like that?&quot;<br /> <br /> I was flabbergasted. Literally, this was like 45 seconds into meeting her for the first time. And I kind of blurted out, &quot;What? You mean surrounded by other human beings? Pretty normal I guess. Why, do you live in the woods with wild animals?&quot;<br /> <br /> Whoops. Needless to say, I was out of a job a few days later, but seriously, fuck her. I filed a complaint with HR but that went nowhere, and I didn't have any kind of proof of what happened so litigation wouldn't have gone anywhere either.<br /> <br /> My next job was at a different school in a different state. And that one I walked out of when the principal threatened to terminate me if I didn't bump up the students grades like all of the other teachers were doing. Again, I reported it to officials who said they'd investigate but the principal is still running that school so nothing got changed.<br /> <br /> And talking with other teachers, these kind of things aren't all that unusual at all. I liked working with the students, but there were too many other elements that just made me feel sick so I quit the profession.<br /> <br /> Educating the next generation is the most important investment that we, as a society, can possible make, but our system royally fucked up, nobody seems to care how vital schools are and so many of the &quot;solutions&quot; are just making things worse. Honestly the whole subjects just depresses the hell out of me.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-30T15:35:41-07:00","id":1085919,"title":"","content":"As a public school teacher of more than 14 years, it's refreshing to see quite a few of you hit the nail on the head when it comes to problems with education in this country. I'd place a heavy emphasis on parental involvement(the lack of it), but there are certainly other factors that go into it as well.<br /> <br /> With that said, there are plenty of success stories--even today. And I happen to work in a school in a lower socio-economic area(but we DO get plenty of funding). My colleagues and I do fear what DeVos and the rest of the &quot;team&quot; have in store for us. Especially considering Florida is already at the bottom of educational funding in general.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#333333\"><span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"font-size:16px\">&quot;Steel isn't strong, boy. Flesh is stronger.&quot;</span></span></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/95-fat-larry","name":"Fat Larry","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar95_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1337"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-30T16:11:21-07:00","id":1085930,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Volke</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1085644-majority-of-u-s-high-schools-aren-t-as-bad-as-it-might-seemed?p=1085900#post1085900\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well I appreciate your thoughts on the matter - I'll be honest and say I didn't know all the facts or look at it like that. It's a shame that things like race are such a large part of the US education system :/, I honestly don't know how racism is still a thing in this day in age. *Shrugs* Maybe I just don't know enough - but I appreciate the perspective Anubis <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></div> </div> </div> </div>The problem is that racism runs deeper than individual beliefs. It's built into the system, so even if folks who genuinely believe their race is superior to others are in a reviled minority nowadays, we still operate under a system biased in favor of those already in power. And as long as people won't acknowledge that racism is still a problem because it isn't overt, the more subtle, pernicious forms persist.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-30T16:32:26-07:00","id":1085942,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1085644-majority-of-u-s-high-schools-aren-t-as-bad-as-it-might-seemed?p=1085930#post1085930\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> The problem is that racism runs deeper than individual beliefs. It's built into the system, so even if folks who genuinely believe their race is superior to others are in a reviled minority nowadays, we still operate under a system biased in favor of those already in power. And as long as people won't acknowledge that racism is still a problem because it isn't overt, the more subtle, pernicious forms persist.</div> </div> </div> </div>Bingo. The American public especially seems too keen to view racism solely as the domain of skinheads, Klansmen, and folks shouting slurs, but the vast majority of racism and the harm it does doesn't take that shape.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Remi. she/her. game designer.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/281-atamajakki","name":"atamajakki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar281_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5487"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-30T17:40:05-07:00","id":1085964,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>atamajakki</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1085644-majority-of-u-s-high-schools-aren-t-as-bad-as-it-might-seemed?p=1085942#post1085942\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Bingo. The American public especially seems too keen to view racism solely as the domain of skinheads, Klansmen, and folks shouting slurs, but the vast majority of racism and the harm it does doesn't take that shape.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, unfortunately like we were saying in the other thread, America doesn't have the idea that someone can be &quot;a little bit&quot; racist. Either you're racist, or you aren't racist. So if someone does something racist, even something fairly incidental, then they're often pilloried just as bad as the skinheads and klansmens are.<br /> <br /> I think it would be nice if society were to somehow accept the idea that there are degrees of racism, and that all of these are bad, but just because you tell a racist joke doesn't make you a neo-Nazi skinhead. It just means you need to stop telling racist jokes and being a jerk.<br /> <br /> But then you have other people who are against that, and feel that allowing for &quot;degrees of racism&quot; simply normalizes casual racism and makes it seem like it's not a big deal. They believe that even the most minor incidents of racism needs to be responded to with the utmost severity because that's the only way to get people to stop doing those sorts of things. If you don't treat minor racism like a big deal, then people won't view it as a big deal. And to be honest, I can't entirely disagree with that line of thought. And yet at the same time, that introduces other problems.<br /> <br /> Man, racism sure is fucking complicated!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":741,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","user":"AnubisXy","date":". 04-30-2017, 09:35 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-30T18:28:17-07:00","id":1085986,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>AnubisXy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1085644-majority-of-u-s-high-schools-aren-t-as-bad-as-it-might-seemed?p=1085964#post1085964\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Yeah, unfortunately like we were saying in the other thread, America doesn't have the idea that someone can be &quot;a little bit&quot; racist. Either you're racist, or you aren't racist. So if someone does something racist, even something fairly incidental, then they're often pilloried just as bad as the skinheads and klansmens are.<br /> <br /> I think it would be nice if society were to somehow accept the idea that there are degrees of racism, and that all of these are bad, but just because you tell a racist joke doesn't make you a neo-Nazi skinhead. It just means you need to stop telling racist jokes and being a jerk.<br /> <br /> But then you have other people who are against that, and feel that allowing for &quot;degrees of racism&quot; simply normalizes casual racism and makes it seem like it's not a big deal. They believe that even the most minor incidents of racism needs to be responded to with the utmost severity because that's the only way to get people to stop doing those sorts of things. If you don't treat minor racism like a big deal, then people won't stop doing those sorts of things. And to be honest, I can't entirely disagree with that line of thought. And yet at the same time, that introduces other problems.<br /> <br /> Man, racism sure is fucking complicated!</div> </div> </div> </div>Personally, I think the key is recognizing that &quot;lesser degree&quot; =/= &quot;not a big deal&quot;. Just because more subtle racist tendencies don't make one a skinhead or a klansman, doesn't mean one shouldn't make an effort to address those tendencies. But recognizing those tendencies exist is an essential step to addressing them, and people <i>will not</i> recognize those tendencies in themselves if doing so means lumping themselves in with skinheads and klansmen","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-30T23:01:21-07:00","id":1086079,"title":"","content":"Should some High School offer entrance exams to get in? I believe these are called magnet schools? I have been reading up on how the schooling system works in Asia, and apparently some of their good high school require entrance exams to get into. I feel that as an U.S citizen there should be more funding into education. I am not saying we neglect broken down schools, but there should be some consideration into offering specialized schools like these. Of course the curriculum of these schools will be a bit more intense, as the focus of these schools would be to feed students into the University system. These schools aren't private though. They would be funded by the district. I think these would be called magnet schools. The school would also look at the student's middle school grades to make a decision.<br /> <br /> There is a similar school in the U.S called Lowell High School in San Francisco, California. The academic achievement of these students are very impressive. One of the reasons why they are doing so well is probably because they are surrounded by intelligent and hard-working peers. The High School is by far the best school in the entire bay area. No school within the proximity can even hold a candle to it. I think we can learn something from this school.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1945,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1945-magicballz","user":"Magicballz","date":". 05-01-2017, 02:59 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1945-magicballz","name":"Magicballz","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"175"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-01T00:58:25-07:00","id":1086097,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Magicballz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1085644-majority-of-u-s-high-schools-aren-t-as-bad-as-it-might-seemed?p=1086079#post1086079\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Should some High School offer entrance exams to get in? I believe these are called magnet schools?</div> </div> </div> </div>Some magnet schools used entrance exams (though it's not a definition of a magnet school).<br /> <br /> For the purposes magnet schools generally are put to use, entrance exams make sense. Magnet schools are frequently &quot;opt in tracking&quot; schools with more specialized focus then general public schools.<br /> <br /> Magnet schools really aren't a solution to most of the larger issues in the US education system though. As long as general public school funding is tied to academic performance in the student body, any program that siphons off the highest performing students is effectively taking quality education (in many different possible forms this thread has already gone over) away from lower performing students.<br /> <br /> There's also a large debate regarding racial diversity and magnet schools. While magnet schools purposefully built to combat white flight are frequently quite diverse, magnet schools that aren't built with this in mind tend to be much less diverse. This creates a sub-group of magnet schools that serve as a way for publicly funded higher quality schools that are in practice not available to minorities.<br /> <br /> The district level funding model continues to make this worse. Poor districts don't have the money to build magnet schools. Rich ones do. Unless the state or federal government is stepping in to fund magnet schools in low property value districts, it just creates more funneling of monetary resources to the people that don't need them.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> There is a similar school in the U.S called Lowell High School in San Francisco, California. The academic achievement of these students are very impressive. One of the reasons why they are doing so well is probably because they are surrounded by intelligent and hard-working peers. The High School is by far the best school in the entire bay area. No school within the proximity can even hold a candle to it. I think we can learn something from this school. </div> </div> </div>I don't really think there's much of a lesson here. Take a school. Build it in a place where it is naturally biased towards students from the two highest scoring ethnic demographic groups, and have exam testing to ensure you're getting the best scoring students from the best scoring demographics... and you get a school that regularly does really well.<br /> <br /> Lowell's use of arena scheduling is an interesting quirk - and one that's very useful for college prep oriented education - but has no confirmed link to increased academic performance.<br /> <br /> Chalking performance up to having high performing peers is also very misleading with the amount of selection bias already present in how the school picks its students. Lowell's continual high performance levels is impossible to separate from the fact that they get to pick their students. You can't demonstrate anything about how education should be done in general from this sort of example.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-01T01:14:07-07:00","id":1086099,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1085644-majority-of-u-s-high-schools-aren-t-as-bad-as-it-might-seemed?p=1086097#post1086097\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Some magnet schools used entrance exams (though it's not a definition of a magnet school).<br /> <br /> For the purposes magnet schools generally are put to use, entrance exams make sense. Magnet schools are frequently &quot;opt in tracking&quot; schools with more specialized focus then general public schools.<br /> <br /> Magnet schools really aren't a solution to most of the larger issues in the US education system though. As long as general public school funding is tied to academic performance in the student body, any program that siphons off the highest performing students is effectively taking quality education (in many different possible forms this thread has already gone over) away from lower performing students.<br /> <br /> There's also a large debate regarding racial diversity and magnet schools. While magnet schools purposefully built to combat white flight are frequently quite diverse, magnet schools that aren't built with this in mind tend to be much less diverse. This creates a sub-group of magnet schools that serve as a way for publicly funded higher quality schools that are in practice not available to minorities.<br /> <br /> The district level funding model continues to make this worse. Poor districts don't have the money to build magnet schools. Rich ones do. Unless the state or federal government is stepping in to fund magnet schools in low property value districts, it just creates more funneling of monetary resources to the people that don't need them.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> I don't really think there's much of a lesson here. Take a school. Build it in a place where it is naturally biased towards students from the two highest scoring ethnic demographic groups, and have exam testing to ensure you're getting the best scoring students from the best scoring demographics... and you get a school that regularly does really well.<br /> <br /> Lowell's use of arena scheduling is an interesting quirk - and one that's very useful for college prep oriented education - but has no confirmed link to increased academic performance.<br /> <br /> Chalking performance up to having high performing peers is also very misleading with the amount of selection bias already present in how the school picks its students. Lowell's continual high performance levels is impossible to separate from the fact that they get to pick their students. You can't demonstrate anything about how education should be done in general from this sort of example.</div> </div> </div> </div>It could help alleviate some of the issues though. You can give students that want to go to college a better chance by putting them into these schools. Use admission requirements like evaluating those students based on their prior grades in middle school. Funding is in question. Though, I think you can offer a suggestion to that.<br /> <br /> Of course, there would still be normal high schools out there for students that aren't that motivated and plan to do something else than to go to some big university. Wouldn't this be a win-win?<br /> So how would this help students in regular high schools? The curriculum in regular high schools can be tailored to the academically challenged. There are some students that have no plans of going to a prestigious college or they aren't as competitive. These students can be given the attention that they need while the ones that are ambitious enough to want to go into university can also succeed as well.<br /> <br /> I read a lot about High School in Asia having entrance exams and admission requirements and it seems to be working pretty well for them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1945,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1945-magicballz","user":"Magicballz","date":". 05-01-2017, 04:54 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1945-magicballz","name":"Magicballz","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"175"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-01T03:22:44-07:00","id":1086121,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Magicballz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1085644-majority-of-u-s-high-schools-aren-t-as-bad-as-it-might-seemed?p=1086099#post1086099\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It could help alleviate some of the issues though.</div> </div> </div> </div>Such as? Enlarging the number of rigorous application magnet schools will do what exactly? How will it help in the context of the rest of the system not changing?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You can give students that want to go to college a better chance by putting them into these schools. </div> </div> </div>Except not everyone that wants to go to college will get into them (or even have one nearby to try to get into, the closest public magnet school to me is around an hour away).<br /> <br /> The demand for higher education for entry level jobs is so high in the US that magnet schools could never be the &quot;go here if you want to go to college&quot; option, because then they'd just be normal schools.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Funding is in question. Though, I think you can offer a suggestion to that. </div> </div> </div>Sure, but that alleviates most of the benefits for magnets schools in the first place: have funding be on a state level instead of a district level, with districts receiving funds per capita, and get rid of test-based means testing for state and federal funding in public schools. Possibly reform administrative positions if you really want to squeeze more money out of the system without raising taxes.<br /> <br /> But if you do that, then there's far less reasons to need magnate schools because they don't have any more resources than normal schools, and normal schools could have test-into programs for college prep.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Of course, there would still be normal high schools out there for students that aren't that motivated and plan to do something else than to go to some big university. </div> </div> </div>And you've yet to make a solid case for the idea that &quot;motivation&quot; is a key factor here, or even a good factor to consider. Teenagers are still maturing and who they are at 16 isn't who they're going to be at 26.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Wouldn't this be a win-win? </div> </div> </div>No. It's a win-lose. It's a lottery where those that get into magnet schools have a huge leg up in getting a good paying job and having a good career, while every other kid has the door to upward social mobility slammed in their face.<br /> <br /> All the potential in kids in regular schools will be wasted because they'll be socially labelled as &quot;academically challenged&quot; for life, instead of just unlucky to not get into the magnet schools. Because they're not going to be in regular school because they're actually academically challenged.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I read a lot about High School in Asia having entrance exams and admission requirements and it seems to be working pretty well for them. </div> </div> </div>There's a huge difference between &quot;achieving intended results,&quot; and &quot;achieving good results.&quot;<br /> <br /> The education models you're looking at are utter hell for all the students that fall between the cracks. If you find out as an adult you're actually not that good in the real world compared to the high-school world? You're boned, because you have no skills for moving into new fields, and aren't competitive for different programs outside what you were tracked into. You might be an awesome chemist, but since you went to school for pre-med instead, you could get stuck working food services because the chemistry programs aren't going to take you and all your talent goes to waste because of choices made when you were still a kid without any real world knowledge of what things look like as a job.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-01T07:29:08-07:00","id":1086187,"title":"","content":"Here's an open question for y'all - how do you feel about the idea of free college education? Would it do anything for lower-performing students? Would it help or hurt the entry-level job market? Does the cost outweigh the benefits?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>On the frontier of the Wild South, there's only one woman with the grit to take on its most dangerous outlaws and bring them <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/984977-back-alive-or-maybe-dead-quest\" target=\"_blank\">Back Alive, or Maybe Dead.</a><br /> <br /> Avatar by <a href=\"http://puzzlinghappenstance.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">K.S. Brenowitz</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","name":"semicasual","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar908_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-01T09:57:53-07:00","id":1086237,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>semicasual</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1085644-majority-of-u-s-high-schools-aren-t-as-bad-as-it-might-seemed?p=1086187#post1086187\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Here's an open question for y'all - how do you feel about the idea of free college education? Would it do anything for lower-performing students? Would it help or hurt the entry-level job market? Does the cost outweigh the benefits?</div> </div> </div> </div>I think in the current US job market, it's necessary. It's so saturated that even traditionally unskilled jobs have their pick of college-educated applicants. A degree is practically a requirement to compete in the job market, and having a basic requirement for entry put you so deep into debt is just unsustainable.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-01T12:39:53-07:00","id":1086288,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>semicasual</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1085644-majority-of-u-s-high-schools-aren-t-as-bad-as-it-might-seemed?p=1086187#post1086187\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Here's an open question for y'all - how do you feel about the idea of free college education? Would it do anything for lower-performing students? Would it help or hurt the entry-level job market? Does the cost outweigh the benefits?</div> </div> </div> </div>I mean, I'm in favor of it. The more people with college education the better. It might hurt the entry-level job market a bit, but overall I think the benefit to having a more educated populace would absolutely be worth it. As a country we <i>want</i> highly educated citizens, and anything to make it easier for people to get an education can only be a good thing.<br /> <br /> At the same time though, I don't think it would help with some of the fundamental problems we're dealing with.In my experience, most people can qualify for student loans and grants. I know people who had bad grades in high school who were still getting 10-12k a year in grants (money they didn't have to pay back), and even people with bad grades could still get loans if they were serious about college. And while taking out tens of thousands of dollars in loans certainly sucks, it's an investment that can pay off. There are, of course, some people who can't get loans or grants at all, but they tend to be in the minority. I think that most people, who actually want to go to college, can already find a way to do so if they're serious about it.<br /> <br /> in that that regard, I don't know that free college would necessarily equate to all that many more people attending and graduating from college.<br /> <br /> Plus, keep in mind that college isn't for everyone. Even if everyone got to go to college for free, quite a few people wouldn't be able to graduate. Everyone gets to go to high school, but there are plenty of people who hit a wall when they start getting to more advanced classes (such as Calculus and the like). So for those people who can't make it through college, even if you made attending free, that wouldn't really help them at all.<br /> <br /> It's also important to consider majors. Getting a bachelors in Accounting or Chemistry is one thing, getting a bachelors in Music or English is something else, and a lot of people graduate college and then find out that their degree isn't worth the paper it's printed on. Then they're forced to compete for lower end jobs against people without college degrees. And that just ends up hurting everyone. The people without college have trouble competing for jobs against people with college, and the people with college are doing a job that they didn't need any college for in the first place.<br /> <br /> So I guess I think free college would be a wonderful thing, but I also don't know that it would have a major effect on many of the fundamental issues we're seeing today involving high school drop out rates, or people struggling to get through college, or issues with employment.<br /> <br /> Though, New York state recently passed a law allowing New Yorkers to attend college for free, and I am extremely interested to see what happens over the next 4 years, and to see how big of a bump NY colleges get as far as enrollment and graduation go. It could be that my guesses and estimations are completely off base. In fact, I rather of hope they are, and NY's new system proves to function great, is adopted by other states in the country and helps bring the US into a new Renaissance. Well, I'll keep my fingers crossed but I won't hold my breath either.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":741,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","user":"AnubisXy","date":". 05-01-2017, 03:44 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-01T13:56:35-07:00","id":1086323,"title":"","content":"While probably not a surprise, I'm very much of the same opinion on AnubisXy on this. It's a good idea, but it isn't a solution to the larger education problem. I agree with the principle, but it can't generate jobs for the graduates with those degrees. It's also, well, the least productive investment in student performance. While this doesn't have to be a zero-sum game, for now the amount of money our country dedicates to education is not enough to cover all the programs that would be awesome to do.<br /> <br /> Free pre-school and increased funding for K-3 education has a drastically better impact on students' long term success than tuition free college would, while there are lots of other measures we can take to reduce the college debt crisis burden. For example, the federal student loan programs could not charge interest, and tie your minimum payments to your income instead of to the amount of the loan. This doesn't eliminate student loan debt, but it does avoid that debt from crippling people economically before they can even find a good job.<br /> <br /> There's a huge number of programs that either need more funding, or need to actually be put in place, that will actually start fixing the US education issues that I would prioritize over free college, while we can address the student debt issues in maybe more complex but less expensive fashions.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-01T14:16:32-07:00","id":1086329,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>semicasual</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1085644-majority-of-u-s-high-schools-aren-t-as-bad-as-it-might-seemed?p=1086187#post1086187\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Here's an open question for y'all - how do you feel about the idea of free college education? Would it do anything for lower-performing students? Would it help or hurt the entry-level job market? Does the cost outweigh the benefits?</div> </div> </div> </div>I think the way things are set up, if used as a solution to the entry-level job market, you would eventually find those same entry-level jobs demanding people with masters rather than bachelors degrees.<br /> <br /> A better public school system, as ambiguous a desire as that is, would be my preference. And finding a way to instill a better work ethic in minors that would help them through the various levels of school.<br /> <br /> It is important for opportunities to be made available in colleges, but without the numbers and just guessing on personal experience, that doesn't <i>seem </i>like as big a problem, beyond the preference for international students. There's a lot of colleges, and community and vocational colleges offer a lot for those not looking for or able to go through the more traditional college education.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-01T23:37:16-07:00","id":1086491,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Magicballz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1085644-majority-of-u-s-high-schools-aren-t-as-bad-as-it-might-seemed?p=1085688#post1085688\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">^ Well how do you explain why many East Asian Americans are so successful in school then? You can still deal with a not so great school with a strong enough work ethics.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://www.pnas.org/content/111/23/8416\" target=\"_blank\">http://www.pnas.org/content/111/23/8416</a><br /> <br /> <b>&quot;The authors wrote that family wealth was not a factor in performance.&quot;</b><br /> <br /> <b>&quot;The poverty rates of Chinese and Vietnamese are higher than they are for whites. However the disadvantaged children of Chinese and Vietnamese immigrant families routinely surpass the educational attainment of their native-born, middle-class white peers.&quot;</b></div> </div> </div> </div>There are some advantages to 1st Generation migrants and 2nd Generation migrants against locals of similar background, at least in the USA. For Asian migrants as a whole that holds 'til 3rd Generation, then they converge almost exactly to the majority average, while for Hispanic migrants it fades by 2nd Generation - <a href=\"http://www.ipr.northwestern.edu/publications/docs/workingpapers/2016/WP-16-10.pdf\" target=\"_blank\">http://www.ipr.northwestern.edu/publ...6/WP-16-10.pdf</a> . There's an advantage for groups which migrate, relative to expected class position, but it fades over the generations.<br /> <br /> That is supplemented by a degree of recent selection for degree holders in the US for certain migrating subgroups. That's quite a new thing in history.<br /> <br /> It's not really a &quot;cultural&quot; effect in the sense of a deep, ethnic or national cultural value placed on education, learning, openness, curiosity, intellectualism, etc. It's more the pattern of selection of migrant groups for people within their groups who are aspirational and view education as &quot;the ladder&quot; in society, them having fewer options other than education in the societies they move to, and in the case of Asian migrants, for people who tend to have higher SES than is typical from where they're from.<br /> <br /> Arguably, the only way to compare the US like-for-like against other countries is to compare the long standing ethnic majority only, because other countries don't have the same history of migration. That does tends to put the US public school system on a fairly high position in the international leaderboards. Anything else is much more complex question.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/929-ghosthead","name":"Ghosthead","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2113"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-06T04:53:42-07:00","id":1088703,"title":"","content":"I coasted through my classes in high school and was woefully unprepared for college. My math and science capabilities were at the level they needed to be. However, my grammar and writing were sub par. High school has one track for college but that track is horribly preparing you for it. In florida, you learn specific parts to pass a pointless standardized tests. It's easy to blame the students but the system is flawed and most teachers don't give a fuck. I certainly don't blame them they have an important job and they get paid dirt for it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/116-epimetheus","name":"Epimetheus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1758"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-06T13:34:03-07:00","id":1088830,"title":"","content":"I don't think most teachers don't give a fuck; in fact quite the opposite. The problem is that the system as is abuses how much teachers care until they're emotionally ground into dust and don't have the motivation to keep banging their heads on the wall of problems the system puts up.<br /> <br /> Despite the fact that US teachers get paid significantly less than other careers that require the same educational and other requirements (last figures I saw were ~15% less), most teachers don't really want fatter pay-checks (not that they'd say no to more money if people wanted to just give them more, but who wouldn't?). Paying them more wouldn't solve the problem of trying to be a good teacher in a system that punishes you for prioritizing what students should know versus getting your students to pass standardized tests. If you asked most teachers if they would rather get paid more, or have more money go into funding their classrooms, they're going to say put the money into the school. Hell it's already a trope that most US public school teachers are expected to spend personal money on classroom expenses. <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}